  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary* from the given one:\n\n$$\n(n>5)\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions. \n\n- Condition I is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n(\\text{ }n>5)\\,⇒\\,(n\\ge 5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nAny number greater than $5$ is greater than or equal to $5.$ So, for $n>5,$ it is true that $n\\ge 5.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n(\\text{ }n>5)\\,⇏\\,(n\\ge 7)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for $n=6,$ it is *not* true that $n\\ge 7.$\n\n- Condition III is a necessary condition: \n\n$$\n(n>5)\\,⇒\\,(n\\ge 3)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nAny number greater than $5$ is greater than $3.$ So, for $n>5,$ it is true that $n\\ge 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
